                <pb facs="0098" n="64" xml:id="pg64"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">No. 17.</note>
                <head>Mr. Geo. H. Yeaman to Mr.
                        Fish.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 255.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United
                                States,</hi><hi rendition="#italic"> Copenhagen</hi></placeName>,
                            <date calendar="gregorian" when="1870-08-13"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >August</hi> 13, 1870</date>. (Received August 30,
                    1870.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Marquis de Cadore left here yesterday,
                    and if he accomplished anything while here, it is wholly unknown, and it is
                    believed that he did not. There can now be no further question about the
                    neutrality of Denmark until the tide of war turns as decidedly in favor of
                    France as it has so far been in favor of Prussia.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>GEO. H. YEAMAN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
